Parts under a permissive licence
--------------------------------

These parts are licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option, so that
plugins built on them may use any licence, closed ones included:

- crates/plugin-api (the plugin contract, including wit/app.wit);
- examples/plugin-template;
- code examples in docs/dev.

See LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE in those directories.

A plugin talks to the server only through this contract and runs as a separate
WebAssembly component. Installing a plugin into RustCloud does not change the
plugin's licence.


Third-party software in the Docker image
----------------------------------------

The image contains ffmpeg and ffprobe, built by docker/build-ffmpeg.sh without
--enable-gpl, so they are under the GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1 or
later. They are separate programs that RustCloud runs; they are not linked into
it. Statically linked into them:

- FFmpeg 8.1.3 (LGPL-2.1-or-later): https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-8.1.3.tar.xz
- dav1d 1.5.1 (BSD-2-Clause): https://code.videolan.org/videolan/dav1d
- OpenH264 2.6.0 (BSD-2-Clause): https://github.com/cisco/openh264
- libwebp (BSD-3-Clause) and zlib (Zlib) from Alpine Linux packages.

docker/build-ffmpeg.sh is the exact recipe: it downloads these sources and
shows every configure option. The image keeps the licence texts of FFmpeg,
dav1d and OpenH264 in /usr/share/doc/rustcloud/third-party/.

The RustCloud binary links Rust crates and bundles a web interface built from
npm packages; they are under permissive licences (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD,
ISC, Zlib, Unicode-3.0, CC0-1.0 and the like). Cargo.lock and
web/package-lock.json list them with their versions.
